The mission of Den Sooluk Program is to establish conditions for the protection and improvement of population health in whole and for each individual, irrespective of social status and gender differences. The starting point of Den Sooluk is to encourage all programme activities and actions through specific and expected improvements in health outcomes. Four programme areas have been selected for which the government has determined the expected outcomes. Improving health outcomes will be in the following areas:
- cardiovascular diseases;
- maternal and child health;
- tuberculosis;
- HIV infection.

The Medium-Term Development Programme (MTDP) of the Kyrgyz Republic for 2012–2014 aims at fostering economic growth by improving national competitiveness, production diversification, job creation, good governance, and strengthened economic integration with the rest of the world. The Programme also plans on expanding local and regional markets for Kyrgyz goods and services by investing in infrastructure projects, establishing “economic districts and growth points” across the country, and removing trade barriers.
The specific objectives include: 
(i) Reform in the public administration and formation of the compact government;
(ii) Reduction of the public expenditures and increasing of revenues; 
(iii) Accelerated development of the economy through start-up of new national projects; 
(iv) Effective public asset management; 
(v) Reform of the license permitting and control-supervisory systems; 
(vi) Reform in the social sphere; reform in the security sphere; 
(vii) Reform in the judicial system.

COVID-19: The Prime Minister has issued its Decision No.31/PM dated 02 April 2020 relating to the policies and measures that will reduce the impact of COVID-19 on the Lao economy. This Decision enacts the below tax and non-tax measures to be effective starting the 02 April 2020: 
1/ Tax exemptions 
• Exemption of Personal Income Tax (Payroll Tax) on salaries of private sector employees and public sector officials who earn less than LAK 5,000,000 (approx. USD 550) for a period of 3 months (April, May, June);
• Exemption of Income Tax for micro-enterprises for a period of 3 months (April, May, June);
• Exemption of customs duty and import taxes for goods imported for COVID-19 prevention purposes such as face masks, hand sanitizers, medical equipment;
2/ Tax deferrals 
• Allow a 3 month delay for paying taxes for companies operating in tourism sector starting April;
• Postpone the year 2019 financial statements submission deadline from 31 March to 30 April;
• Postpone the payment of road tax fee from 31 March to 30 April;
• Postpone the payment of Social Security contribution for companies impacted by COVID-19 for a period of 3 months (April, May, June);
3/ Other measures 
• Allow a reduction and a delay in payment of electricity and water bills for private households and business enterprises;
• Agree for Bank of Lao PDR to reduce the standard interest rate and the regulatory provision ratio of commercial banks;
• Agree for Bank of Lao PDR to implement with the commercial banks a revised credit policy that will support the companies impacted by COVID-10 which will include the postponement of capital and interest payment, reduction of interest rate, provision of new loans, debt improvement and loan classification;
Prime Minister assigns all Ministries involved in the above measures to issue the necessary regulations for this new policy to be implemented and mobilize the government officials in estimating the impact of COVID-19 on both public sector and private sector, in particular for tourism sector recovery, macro-economic stability, public revenue (taxes) collection, government expenditures, goods production and exports.

Overall architecture and strategies for implementing health reform: To reach targets, directions and goals set up especially reaching universal health coverage in Lao PDR by 2020/25, health reform will be implemented in 3 phases such as: 
•	Phase I (2013-2015): focuses on the achievement of health related MDG and lays out a solid foundation for universal access to essential health services; 
•	Phase II (2016-2020): aims to ensure that essential health services with reasonable good quality are available and accessible to, and used by a majority of the people; 
•	Phase III (2021-2025): expects to achieve universal health coverage with an adequate service benefit package and appropriate financial protection for a vast majority of the population... 
On 9 August 2013, the Strategy Health Sector Reform by 2020 is approved by the Government. The Goals by 2020 includes the nutrition focus as: 
•	The proportion of underweight in children under 5 year of age targeted at 15%; 
•	The proportion of stunned children under 5 year of age targeted at 28%; 
•	Infant mortality rate targeted at 30/1 000 live births and Under 5 mortality rate targeted at 45/1 000 live births; 
•	Maternal mortality ratio targeted at 200/100 000 live births; 
•	Proportion of the population with sustainable access to clean water targeted at 90% and the population with access to latrines targeted at 80%; 
The Nutrition national program: supplement food, breast feeding, iron and acid folic distribution, vitamin A distribution, distribution of deworming tablets, distribution of iodinized salt, nutrition education.

