The Ministry of Environment, Waters and Forests as responsible authority, in December 2015 developed a Strategic Sectorial Plan. That includes forests, and respectively a Forests Priority Actions Plan. The priority objectives for the responsible management of the forests have been set as:
I. For 2016:
1. Reaching the objectives stated in the EU Regulation 995 / 2010 (EUTR);
2. Developing the Wood Tracking & SUMAL applications as efficient control instruments, increasing transparency of the information generated and the development of a series of inspection monitoring and record-keeping systems;
3. Adopting the guide for sectorial participative strategic planning with the purpose of elaborating the National Forestry Strategy;
4. Setting up a structure specialised in forestry inspections, to function within the responsible public central authority under the forestry division;
5. Instituting criteria, performance and conduct indicators and monitoring measures for Forest Guard personnel through the organisation and functioning normative act;
6. Modifying and completing the Capitalisation Regulation for the wood mass from the forest fund in public property (Governmental Decision 924/2015), so that it adequately transposes the principles of capitalisation instituted in the Forest Code;
7. Modification of the 171/2010 law for forest contraventions in order to fully cover with sanctioning measures the deeds that constitute forest contraventions;
8. The execution of the legal framework that would ensure compensation for the equivalent of the products that the forest owners do not harvest, due to the protection functions established through forest management plans for their properties that determine restrictions in harvesting timber;
9. Instituting the „National Catalogue of Virgin and Quasi-virgin Forests” (according to the Forest Code art. 26. par. 3) as an official instrument for recording and managing in order to ensure their strict
protection;
10. The establishment of the „National Forestry Council”.

Within the framework of the Common Agricultural Policy (CAP), the European Union (EU) has implemented a Common Rural Development policy. The main legislation concerned with rural development policy is Council Regulation (EC) No. 1698/2005. The purpose of the Common Rural Development policy for 2007 – 2013 is improving the competitiveness of the agricultural and forestry sector; improving the environment and the countryside, and improving the quality of life in rural areas and encouraging diversification of the rural economy. For 2007-2013, the budget for the European Agricultural Fund for Rural Development (EAFRD), which is the main instrument for financing the rural development activities, was set at €96.3 billion. 
Countries within the EU will set out their own rural development programmes, and must spread their funding across all three of these themes in order to ensure a balanced approach to policy. Another requirement of the rural development policy is that some of the funding must support projects based on experience with the Leader Community Initiatives, which sets a focus on establishing partnerships with rural actors. The emphasis for the rural development policy is a coherent strategy across the EU as a whole. This is being achieved through the use of National Strategy plans, which must be based on EU Strategic Guidelines. 
This approach should help to:
identify the areas where the use of EU support for rural development adds the most value at EU level;
make the link with the main EU priorities (for example, those set out under the Lisbon and Göteborg agendas);
ensure consistency with other EU policies, in particular those for economic cohesion and the environment;
assist the implementation of the new market-oriented CAP and the necessary restructuring it will entail in countries of the EU.

Commission Regulation (EU) No 1255/2010: 

The following tariff quotas are hereby opened yearly for the period from 1 January to 31 December:
(a) 9400 tonnes of "baby beef", expressed in carcase weight, originating in Croatia;
(b) 1500 tonnes of "baby beef", expressed in carcase weight, originating in Bosnia and Herzegovina;
(c) 1650 tonnes of "baby beef", expressed in carcase weight, originating in the former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia;
(d) 8700 tonnes of "baby beef", expressed in carcase weight, originating in Serbia;
(e) 800 tonnes of "baby beef", expressed in carcase weight, originating in Montenegro

The customs duty applicable under the quotas referred to above shall be 20 % of the ad valorem duty and 20 % of the specific duty as laid down in the Common Customs Tariff.

The government approved the Strategy for Activities in the Field of Hydrometeorology and related areas for the period up to 2030 (taking into account aspects of climate change) (hereinafter - the Strategy).

The Strategy is aimed at ensuring the implementation of the Concept for the Long-Term Socio-Economic Development of Russia for the period until 2020 in terms of information support for protecting the population from natural and man-made emergencies, preserving and protecting the natural environment, and increasing the efficiency of weather-dependent sectors of the economy (water sector, agricultural sector, transport, energy and other industries).

The main objective of the Strategy is the formation of a highly effective hydrometeorological service that ensures the fulfillment of the functions of providing consumers with timely and reliable hydrometeorological and heliogeophysical information, information about the state of the environment, its pollution; on the effective and safe conduct of work related to the active impact on meteorological and other geophysical processes, based on modern equipment and technologies.

The strategy determines the main directions of development of activities in the field of hydrometeorology and related fields (meteorology, climatology, agrometeorology, hydrology, oceanology, heliogeophysics, the area of ​​active impacts on meteorological and other geophysical processes), environmental monitoring, its pollution, including the ionosphere and near-Earth outer space, as well as in the field of providing information on the state of the environment, its pollution and dangerous natural phenomena.

The strategy will be implemented in three stages. The implementation plan for the first stage of the Strategy has been approved. The main task of the first stage is the modernization of computing facilities, archiving facilities, communication systems and the state monitoring network.

The plan provides for:

- development of an observational network;

- development of basic technologies for processing and disseminating observations, predicting the state of the environment and its pollution;

- development of the Unified State Fund of data on the state of the environment, its pollution and other data funds;

- solution of applied problems;

- development and implementation of models and methods for assessing the economic effect of hydrometeorological support for the socio-economic development of Russia;

- development of a system of relations between participants in activities in the field of hydrometeorology and related fields;

- Fulfillment of international obligations of the Russian Federation, expansion of areas and forms of international cooperation in the field of hydrometeorology and related fields.

The document was submitted by the Russian Ministry of Labor in order to fulfill the instructions of the President of the Russian Federation and to ensure the preparation and submission to the State Duma of draft related federal laws.

By the Decree of the President of the Russian Federation dated May 7, 2012 No. 597  “On measures to implement the state social policy”, the Government was instructed to develop a draft Strategy for the long-term development of the pension system, providing for a mechanism to increase the size of the pension to citizens who expressed their intention to continue working after reaching retirement age and made a decision to postpone the appointment of a pension, as well as defining measures guaranteeing the safety of pension savings and ensuring a return on their investment.

The decree approves the Strategy for the Long-Term Development of the Pension System of the Russian Federation (hereinafter - the Strategy).

The need to prepare the Strategy is determined by the economic and demographic challenges facing the domestic pension insurance system.

The main objectives of the development of the pension system are as follows:

guaranteeing a socially acceptable level of pension provision;
ensuring the balance and long-term financial stability of the pension system.
The implementation of the Strategy will allow to solve the following tasks:

providing a replacement rate for a retirement pension of lost earnings up to 40% with normative length of service and average wages in the Russian Federation;
achievement of an acceptable level of pensions for the middle class through participation in corporate and private pension systems (additionally at least 15% of the replacement ratio);
ensuring an average old-age labor pension of at least 2.5–3 living wages of a pensioner (in 2030, at least 3 PMP);
maintaining an acceptable level of insurance burden for economic entities with a single insurance premium rate for all categories of employers;
ensuring the balance of formed pension rights with the sources of their financing;
development of a three-level pension system for groups with different incomes (for medium- and high-income categories - relying on voluntary pension insurance and non-state pension provision);
increasing the effectiveness of the funded component of the pension system.
In addition, the Strategy provides for the reform of the institution of early retirement pensions and it is proposed for employers who have jobs with special working conditions to establish an additional insurance premium rate to the Pension Fund of the Russian Federation.

Based on the analysis of the problems identified in the functioning of the funded component of the pension system, a set of measures is proposed to improve it:

redistribution of the insurance premium rate with the provision to insured persons who opted for the formation of pension savings funds, the right to choose the formation of pension savings funds in a non-state pension fund or management company in the amount of 2% or 6% of the insurance premium rate for the funded component of the pension system;
creation of a multi-level system of guarantees for the preservation of pension savings, including a system of integrated guarantee funds;
expanding the list of instruments for investing pension savings funds;
expanding the composition of financial institutions allowed to participate in the formation of pension savings by including insurance companies and credit organizations.
In order to build a full-fledged pension system, the Strategy provides for the determination of the foundations for the creation and functioning of corporate pension systems, as well as the necessary conditions for employers to provide corporate pension benefits for their employees and the principles of state control over these activities.

To improve the formation of pension rights in the system, the Strategy envisages a transition to a new formula for calculating labor pensions based on the standard length of service (35 years), as well as a special procedure for calculating disability pensions and survivors, reflecting the socially significant nature of this type of security.

In order to stimulate longer working hours for citizens who have decided to work after reaching retirement age and to delay the appointment of a pension, the Strategy proposes to provide for its establishment in a higher amount.

The government approved the Transport Strategy of the Russian Federation for the period until 2030 (hereinafter - the Strategy).

The strategy defines the goals and priorities for the development of transport for the long term. The main goal of the development of the transport system is to satisfy the needs of innovative socially oriented development of the economy and society in competitive high-quality transport services.

Priorities for the development of the transport system:

- the formation of a single transport space in Russia on the basis of the balanced development of an efficient transport infrastructure;

- ensuring the availability, volume and competitiveness of transport services for cargo owners in accordance with the needs of innovative development of the country's economy;

- ensuring the availability and quality of transport services for the population in accordance with social standards;

- integration into the global transport space and the realization of the country's transit potential;

- improving the security of the transport system;

- reduction of the harmful effects of transport on the environment;

- development of transport equipment, technologies and information support.

The main mechanisms for implementing the Strategy are federal and regional target programs. The composition and structure of these programs should correspond to the main targets, the formed goals, objectives and mechanisms of the Strategy.

The strategy is supposed to be implemented in two stages.

The first stage (until 2015) is the completion of the modernization of the transport system using targeted investment methods, the elimination of “bottlenecks” and the transition to its systemic integrated development in all key areas. At this stage, it is planned to implement the federal target program “Development of the Transport System of Russia (2010–2015)”, which includes five subprograms formed according to the industry principle (“Railway Transport”, “Highways”, “Sea Transport”, “Inland Water Transport” and “ Civil Aviation").

The second stage (2016 - 2030) is the innovative development of the transport system in all directions. The main mechanism for implementing the Strategy at this stage will be federal targeted programs for the development of the transport system for five-year periods.

