The state policy on managing the land fund of Russia is aimed at creating and improving legal, economic, social and organizational conditions for the development of land relations.

The signed order approved the Fundamentals of the state policy on the use of the land fund in the Russian Federation for 2012-2017 (hereinafter - the Fundamentals).

The objectives of the Fundamentals are to increase the efficiency of use and protect land as the main component of the environment and the main means of production in agriculture while ensuring the food security of the country.

The main objectives of the state policy on land management:

- creating conditions for organizing the rational and efficient use of land, which includes taking into account social and industry needs, the requirements for sustainable development of territories, as well as compliance with guarantees of the rights of participants in land relations;

- ensuring the protection of nature and the environment, including the protection of land and the preservation of cultural heritage;

- preservation and improvement of the quality of land;

- maintaining the status of protected natural territories as protected lands as part of the land fund;

- providing conditions for increasing the efficiency of civil turnover of land, including those aimed at protecting rights to real estate, as well as to reduce administrative barriers and ensure taxation of real estate.

The fundamentals, in particular, provide for the improvement of the procedure for determining the legal regime of land plots by excluding from the land legislation the principle of dividing land for the intended purpose into categories; providing guarantees of land rights and protecting the rights and legitimate interests of owners, land users, landowners and tenants of land plots; improvement of state land supervision and municipal land control; development of state land monitoring.

The Government of Russia has implemented a program called "Support and Development of Meat Cattle Farming" (sub-program of the State Program 2013-2020). The Government has allocated 7.45 billion rubles (US$124.000.000) for the following:
- Subsidies for support of purebred meat cattle farming;
- Subsidies for support of economically important provincial programs in meat cattle;
- Subsidies for support of economically important provincial programs in meat cattle.

The concept of the demographic policy of the Russian Federation for the period until 2025 was approved by Decree of the President of Russia of October 9, 2007 No. 1351 (hereinafter - the Concept).

The implementation of stage II of the Concept was completed in 2015.

As of January 1, 2016, the population in Russia is 146.5 million people. Life expectancy was 71.39 years (in 2007 - 67.61, growth - 3.78 years), the total fertility rate in Russia - 1.784 (in 2007 - 1.416, growth - by 20.6%). The number of births per year increased by 8.6% - from 1,788.9 million people in 2010 to 1,942.7 million people in 2014 (by 153.8 thousand children). The number of deaths decreased by 11.8% - from 2166.7 thousand people in 2006 to 1911.4 thousand people in 2015.

These results are due, inter alia, to the implementation of a set of measures for state support of the family in connection with the birth and upbringing of children.

In 2016, the government approved the action plan for the implementation of stage III of the Concept.

Since 2010, Russia has seen a decrease in the number of women of childbearing age. In 2014, there was a decrease of 2.5 million people in the number of women aged 15–49 years (6.6%). The reduction in the number of women of young ages (20–29 years), which accounted for approximately 60% of all births, amounted to 1.4 million people.

At the III stage of implementation of the Concept, measures are provided for proactive response to a possible deterioration of the demographic situation in the country.

The duration of stage III is 10 years. The stage is divided into two periods: 2016–2020 and 2021–2025. This division is due to the fact that the planned horizon of government programs is currently limited to 2020. Accordingly, the action plan for the implementation of the Concept was drawn up for the first period of stage III - from 2016 to 2020.

The plan provides for the implementation of 50 measures aimed at:

- reduction of preventable causes of death;

- ensuring comprehensiveness, increasing the availability and effectiveness of medical care for elderly citizens, including the development of geriatric services;

- providing conditions for protecting the health of the population of the Russian Federation from the consequences of tobacco and alcohol consumption;

- the implementation of a set of measures to improve physical education and sports work with the population;

- development and implementation of the state program “Safe Work” for 2017–2025;

- the development of additional mechanisms for economic stimulation of the birth of second and subsequent children;

- support for young women and young families in order to increase the number of births at an earlier age;

- increasing the availability of housing for families with children;

- regulation of migration in accordance with the socio-economic needs of the Russian Federation.

According to the results of the implementation of the plan's activities, by 2020 the population is expected to increase to 147.5 million people; increase in life expectancy up to 74 years; increase in the total birth rate to the level of 1.87; ensuring migration growth of at least 200 thousand people annually.

The Strategy for sustainable development of rural territories of the Russian Federation for the period until 2030 was prepared by the Ministry of Agriculture in pursuance of an order from the President of Russia following a meeting of the State Council and the Council on the implementation of priority national projects and demographic policies on April 21, 2014 ( No. Pr-995GS of May 1, 2014, subparagraph “a” of paragraph 1 ).

The signed decree approves the Strategy for Sustainable Development of Rural Territories of the Russian Federation for the period up to 2030 (hereinafter - the Strategy).

The strategy was developed in accordance with the Federal Law “On Strategic Planning in the Russian Federation” .

Strategy Objectives:

- Creation of favorable socio-economic conditions for rural areas to fulfill their socially significant functions and to solve the tasks of territorial development;

- ensuring stabilization of the rural population and creating conditions for its growth by reducing mortality, increasing life expectancy, reducing the migration outflow of the population;

- providing employment, improving the level and quality of life of the rural population, taking into account modern requirements and standards;

- improving the efficiency of agriculture and the contribution of rural territories to the socio-economic development of the country.

The strategy provides for two stages of its implementation: the transitional stage (2015–2020), the main stage (2021–2030).

Priorities of the transitional stage of the Strategy implementation: the formation of mechanisms and incentives for stabilizing the demographic situation, ensuring the development of housing, engineering and road transport infrastructure, adjusting existing imbalances in ensuring the accessibility and quality of education and health services, ensuring epizootic well-being, developing and implementing a system of social standards, improvement of legal regulation to solve the identified problems of sustainable development of rural areas s, the formation of effective mechanisms for inter-agency cooperation and coordination to achieve sustainable rural development at the federal, regional and local levels.

At the main stage, the development of rural infrastructure will continue along with the intensification of activities to improve the quality of life of rural residents. The priorities of this stage are the formation and implementation of a balanced system of mechanisms and incentives to improve the demographic situation, taking into account regional specifics, promoting the introduction of new cost-effective mechanisms of economic activity and the provision of social services, accelerated development of communications and communications infrastructure, comprehensive provision of cultural and leisure needs of the population.

The main mechanisms for implementing the Strategy are the state programs of the Russian Federation and state programs of the constituent entities of the Federation.

Achieving the goals and objectives of the Strategy is determined by the achievement by 2030 of the main targets, including:

- stabilization of the rural population at the level of 35 million people;

- increase the expected life expectancy of the rural population to 75.6 years;

- reducing the migration outflow of the rural population to 74.1 thousand people;

- ensuring the average annual growth rate of agricultural production in the amount of 5.5%;

- increase the level of employment of the rural population to 65.5%;

- expanding the network of feldsher-midwife centers and (or) offices of general practitioners in rural areas by 1.7 thousand units;

- increase the share of the rural population, systematically engaged in physical education and sports, up to 37.3%;

- increase in the proportion of rural settlements that have a connection on paved roads with a network of roads, up to 80%.

The document was developed by the Ministry of Labor of Russia in accordance with the order of the President of Russia following a meeting of the Presidium of the State Council on the development of a system of social protection for elderly citizens on August 5, 2014 (No. Pr-2159 of September 9, 2014, subparagraph “a” of paragraph 1) . The government approved the Action Strategy in the interests of senior citizens until 2025 (hereinafter - the Strategy).

The strategy defines the goals, principles, tasks and priority areas of state social policy in relation to citizens of the older generation.

The key objectives of the Strategy are a steady increase in the duration, level and quality of life of older citizens, and the promotion of their active longevity.

Priority areas of the Strategy:

- stimulation of employment of elderly citizens;

- increasing the level of their financial literacy;

- ensuring the access of elderly citizens to information and educational resources;

- development of modern forms of social services, improvement of the health care system, development of the social services market, protection of the rights of senior citizens;

- the formation of conditions for organizing leisure activities for older people;

- application of a differentiated approach to determining the forms of social support for older citizens.

The number of senior citizens in Russia is growing, and, according to the forecasts of the Federal State Statistics Service, by 2021 their share in the total population will be 26.7% (39.5 million people).

The draft order on approval of the new version of the Transport Strategy of the Russian Federation for the period up to 2030 (hereinafter referred to as the Transport Strategy) was prepared by the Ministry of Transport of Russia pursuant to decisions taken at a meeting of the Government of the Russian Federation on August 27, 2013 .

The transport strategy was approved by Government Decree of November 22, 2008 No. 1734-r . It defines the main directions of state transport policy for the long term, including the goals of the development of the transport system and the principles of state regulation of transport activity. The transport strategy covers all types of transport (road, rail, air, sea and inland waterways), as well as road facilities.

The main task of the state in the sphere of functioning and development of the transport system of Russia is to create conditions for economic growth, increase the competitiveness of the national economy and the quality of life of the population through access to safe and high-quality transport services, and turn the geographical features of Russia into its competitive advantage.

The actual implementation of the Transport Strategy began in 2009.

Over the past period, within the framework of the Transport Strategy, large-scale projects have been implemented in all sectors of transport and in the road sector. At the same time, issues have accumulated, the solution of which requires amendments to the Transport Strategy, including in connection with the need to introduce new innovative technologies and changes in the volume of financing of projects in the field of transport.

The new edition of the Transport Strategy was developed taking into account the Russian Federation Innovative Development Strategy for the period until 2020 , the Development Strategy of the Russian Arctic and national security in the period until 2020, Strategies for the development of the shipbuilding industry for the period up to 2020 and beyond, Strategies for the development of transport engineering in the Russian Federation in 2007-2010 and for the period up to 2015, Strategies for the development of the aviation industry for the period up to 2015, Strategies for the development of the automotive industry of the Russian Federation for the period until 2020 and other sectoral strategies in the field of industry, energy, forestry and agriculture, strategies for socio-economic development of Russian regions.

The objectives of the new edition of the Transport Strategy:

- the formation of a single transport space in Russia on the basis of a balanced accelerated development of an efficient transport infrastructure;

- ensuring the availability and quality of transport and logistics services in the field of freight transportation at the level of development needs of the country's economy;

- ensuring the availability and quality of transport services for the population in accordance with social standards;

- integration into the global transport space, the implementation of the country's transit potential;

- improving the security of the transport system;

- reduction of the negative impact of the transport system on the environment.

To achieve these goals, the following basic tasks are foreseen:

- the formation and maintenance of the country's transport and economic balance and the planning of the development of transport infrastructure based on it;

- balanced development of an integrated infrastructure of transport communications of all types of transport;

- development of large transport hubs, logistics distribution centers, dry ports and terminals in the main directions of transportation and at the junctions between modes of transport and ensuring their uniform technological compatibility;

- elimination of gaps and bottlenecks of the transport network, limiting its throughput;

- increase in throughput and speed parameters of the transport infrastructure;

- the development of innovative technologies for the construction, reconstruction and maintenance of transport infrastructure;

- updating and increasing fleets of freight rolling stock based on the transport and economic balance;

- creating a market for competitive integrated transport and logistics services;

- improvement of procedures for admission to commercial transport activities;

- Creation of intelligent transport systems using the GLONASS global navigation system and modern information and telecommunication technologies, information standards and unified transportation documents (ensuring the implementation of highly efficient logistics transport technologies);

- ensuring the availability of transport services for the transport of goods in the Far North, Siberia, the Far East and remote regions of Russia, including along the Northern Sea Route;

- the development of inland water transport and technologies that ensure the switching of cargo flows to it during the navigation period;

- planning the development of passenger transport systems and rolling stock based on the forecast of socio-economic development and transport and economic balance;

- development of high-speed and high-speed rail passenger transportation;

- the formation of an economic and legal model of a competitive market for affordable and high-quality transportation services for the population;

- development of regional air transportation;

- Creation of competitive transport corridors based on technically and technologically integrated transport and logistics infrastructure, as well as systems for coordinating business processes for supply;

- integration into the international transport space;

- improving the safety of traffic, flights and shipping;

- ensuring the state of protection of transport infrastructure objects from acts of unlawful interference;

- ensuring the activities of specialized emergency services at a level consistent with international and national requirements;

- modernization of vehicles and transport infrastructure facilities aimed at reducing their negative impact on the environment;

- increase the share of the use of environmentally friendly fuels, hybrid and electric engines of vehicles, materials and technologies;

- improving the energy efficiency of transport.

In accordance with the decisions adopted at the Government meeting on August 27, 2013, the new version of the Transport Strategy adjusted the implementation terms and stages of work for individual projects, including projects for the development of high-speed rail traffic, as well as updated the action plan for the implementation of the Transport Strategy for the medium term period (2014–2018).

The new version of the Transport Strategy will contribute to the solution of systemic socio-economic problems, such as:

- the presence of territorial and structural imbalances in the development of transport infrastructure;

- insufficient level of accessibility of transport services for the population, labor mobility;

- insufficient quality of transport services;

- low level of export of transport services, including the use of transit potential;

- insufficient level of transport security;

- strengthening the negative impact of transport on the environment.

The government approved the Development Strategy for the food and processing industry for the period up to 2020 (hereinafter - the Strategy).

The purpose of the Strategy is to ensure a guaranteed and sustainable supply of safe and quality food to the country's population.

The strategy is based on the following indicators:

- the share of domestic agricultural products, raw materials and food in the total volume of commodity resources of the domestic market;

- rational food consumption standards that meet modern requirements for a healthy diet and the projected population of the country until 2020;

- prospects for the balance of production and consumption of the main types of agricultural products, raw materials and food.

According to the Strategy, the dynamics of the development of food and processing industries for the period until 2020 is determined by various factors, which include the demand for certain types of food from various social groups, the growth of incomes of the population, Russia's entry into the world economic space and an increase in the likelihood of risks that threaten sustainable and the dynamic development of the food industry by the global food market.

The strategy is supposed to be implemented in two stages, due to the development of the agro-industrial complex and the possibility of attracting investment in the modernization of industry.

At the same time, the priorities of the medium and long term are highlighted.

The priorities of the medium-term period, in particular, include:

- development of the raw material base of crop products and livestock products;

- technical re-equipment of organizations with the introduction of modern scientific and technological progress to reduce energy consumption, reduce harmful emissions into the environment;

- development of competition, cooperation, integration relations and the formation of product sub-complexes, territorial clusters, the introduction of new technical regulations and standards.

Long-Term Priorities:

- development of import-substituting food and processing industries, including meat, dairy, sugar and fish industries;

- the transition of food and processing industries to resource-saving technologies that ensure non-waste production and production with minimal environmental impact;

- processing of new types of raw materials obtained using bio- and nanotechnology.

The decisions taken will contribute to the creation of the necessary conditions for the modernization of industry, the implementation of the goals of socio-economic development of Russia until 2020.

This revised strategy indicates that Rwanda can reach total export revenues of USD 90 million for the year 2012 and at the same time more than double the average revenue for tea farmers. To do this, Rwanda needs to move from producing almost solely bulk black tea, the majority of which is sold at auction, to producing better quality teas, diversified teas, and branded Rwandan packaged teas, which are sold directly to buyers with whom strong relationships have been built.
This vision is supported by five strategic pillars that highlight the priority areas for action to continue and accelerate the transformation of the tea sector. These strategic pillars have been agreed by the industry cluster working group and tackle key constraints to growth at each stage of the value chain. These pillars, which must be built on the basis of restructuring Rwanda’s key tea institutions, especially OCIR-Thé after privatization, will focus Rwanda’s tea industry on:
• Raising yields to increase tea growers’ income and export revenues;
• Investing in factories to ensure sufficient capacity to process all green leaf;
• Improving quality to avoid bulk market price decline;
• Developing a gradual migration path to a higher-value product; and
• Creating a Rwandan tea brand to signal quality and engaging in targeted marketing.

The policy seeks to increase investment, achieve well-managed protected areas, and provide essential visitor facilities and services to increase visitation and employment creation.
Policy Objectives: 
 Raise investment in protected areas by the private sector or Public-Private Partnerships (PPPs); 
 Streamline proper management and conservation of protected areas through concessions; 
 Provide quality visitor/tourist infrastructure, facilities & services within the protected areas; 
 Generate revenues to the government that can be channelled for management and conservation of these protected areas; 
 Promote sustainable tourism that generates employment and reduces poverty

The main objective of the Rwandan Energy Sector Strategic Plan (ESSP) is to ensure effective delivery of the set targets in the energy sector as set out under the EDPRS-II and to guide in the implementation of the National Energy Policy (EP). The ESSP thus functions as a detailed plan that serves to translate the policy directives and principles into concrete measures necessary to reach medium-term targets, reflecting current resource constraints and risk/uncertainties. The ESSP also contains high-level target objectives, and a monitoring framework with key performance indicators and interim milestones.
The ESSP addresses the challenges facing the sector and outlines strategies to meet critical needs. Principal among these needs are:
i. To enhance energy security and to better align demand and supply balances;
ii. To ensure that tariffs and prices for energy services are cost-reflective;
iii. To reduce coordination failures and the cost of financing infrastructure;
iv. To meet energy access targets without raising the average cost of electricity services, increasing subsidies, compromising service quality, or compromising environmental sustainability;
v. To develop the requisite institutional, organizational, and human capacity to increase accountability, transparency, national ownership and decentralized implementation of energy service delivery;
vi. To dramatically scale-up energy investments through more effective private sector engagement.

The vision of the Government in agricultural policy is oriented towards the following major axis:
• Food security through the creation of an environment favourable to income generation;
• Modern, professional, innovative and specialised agriculture;
• A strict market oriented agriculture (domestic, sub-regional, regional and international);
• Fair distribution of benefits from all products resulting from different stages of processing;
• Integrated and diversified agriculture:
• Agriculture integrated in animal husbandry;
• Agriculture and livestock integrated in rural development.
• Agriculture friendly to the environment.
Global objective of agricultural policy: To create conditions favourable to sustainable development and promotion of agricultural and livestock produces, in order to ensure national food security, integration of agriculture and livestock in a market-oriented economy and to generate increasing incomes to the producers.
Key sub-sectors
• Agriculture;
• Animal Resources;
• Soil and water Management

The goal of this Policy is to conserve Rwanda’s biological diversity, to sustain the integrity, health and productivity of its ecosystems and ecological processes, whilst providing lasting development benefits to the nation through the ecologically sustainable, socially equitable, and economically efficient use of biological resources.
The Purpose of this policy is to: provide an overarching framework for the conservation, sustainable utilization, access to biodiversity resources and fair and equitable sharing of benefits derived from the resources.

Policy Objectives: 
Provide a comprehensive and cohesive policy framework that will strengthen the Government’s ability to conserve and protect Rwanda’s natural and cultural resources
Provide a legal and institutional framework for biodiversity conservation and management throughout the country;
Promote partnerships, incentives and benefit sharing to enhance biodiversity conservation and management;
Promote generation, management of conservation knowledge, including traditional knowledge, and its application in the conservation of biodiversity;
Provide a framework for access to genetic resources and the sharing of benefits derived from those resources;
Promote positive attitudes towards biodiversity conservation and management.

Vision: “All Rwandan school children shall achieve their full development potential, by studying in a healthy environment in child-friendly schools, free from disease, prejudice and violence”.
Objectives
Short-term
- To provide preventive and curative services that address needs of school children;
- To ensure provision of safe water and adequate sanitation facilities in schools;
- To improve and enhance knowledge of students and teachers about SH, including sexual and reproductive health and GBV, prevention of HIV and AIDS and other diseases, management of disabilities and special learning needs, hygiene, nutrition, physical education and mental health.
Medium-term
- To improve attendance and completion rates, due to better health status of children;
- To increase access to education, especially for girls, OVC’s and other disadvantaged children;
- To create a healthy, safer and hygienic environment for the school community, so as to ensure effective teaching and learning for both teachers and students.
Long-term
- To improve secondary graduation rates of students;
- To improve preparedness of students for tertiary opportunities;
- To have a healthier and better qualified workforce, and responsible parents for the next generations;
- To decrease medical health care costs, due to prevention and early detection of diseases in school children;
- To improve equality, equity and reconciliation.

The overall objective of the ENR Sector Strategic Plan is to ensure that environment and natural resources are utilised and managed productively in support of equitable and sustained national development in line with the EDPRS targets and Vision 2020 aspirations.
Specific objectives:
1. To promote equitable, productive and sustainable use and management of land resources by controlling land degradation, enhancing security of tenure and improving productivity and administration through technological and market-based systems;
2. To ensure that the country’s water resources are conserved and sustainably managed in an integrated and equitable manner for the continued and balanced support to economic, social and ecological needs at local, national and transboundary levels;
3. To ensure adequate and sustainable supply of forest and biomass resources to meet the growing multiple demands for food, fibre, fodder, fuel as well as environmental services, by increasing forest and tree cover, improving utilisation efficiency and promoting alternatives to wood and non wood forest and tree products;
4. To promote productive, equitable and environmentally friendly utilisation of the country’s mineral and other earth resources, by promoting efficient and value-adding technologies, raising awareness and skills of key actors about the consequences of unsustainable mining, and options for sustainable minerals exploitation;
5. To restore, conserve and sustainably manage key ecosystems to ensure continued and enhanced integrity and functioning of Rwanda’s diverse ecosystems in terms of productive and regulatory services provided at local, national and international levels;
6. To promote sustainable development by raising awareness of and integrating environment and natural resource management principles and techniques across all key social and productive sectors of the EDPRS;
7. To strengthen policy and legislative frameworks for sustainable management of environment and natural resources, by harmonising policies, legal and regulatory instruments within/ across sectors and with regional and international frameworks; as well as putting in place incentives for effective implementation and oversight;
8. To increase human and institutional capacity, at national and decentralised entities, within civil society and private sector, as well as community levels, for effective planning, coordination, implementation and monitoring of ENR activities;

